[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 475,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 432,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67394,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 432,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cardiac fibroma is a benign mesenchymal tumour, composed of bland fibroblasts in a variably collagenized stroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 476,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 433,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67395,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 433,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8810/0 Fibroma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 477,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 434,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67396,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 434,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F01 &amp; XH8E66 Benign neoplasm of intrathoracic organs &amp; Fibroma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 478,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 435,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67397,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 435,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> cardiac fibromatosis; fibrous hamartoma; fibroelastic hamartoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 479,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 436,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67398,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 436,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67399,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Nearly all cardiac fibromas arise in the ventricular septum; for the remaining cases, the most common site is the ventricular free wall (localization in the left ventricle is 5&nbsp;times as common as in the right ventricle), followed by the right and left atria {25019440}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67400,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Due to the predilection of cardiac fibroma for the ventricular septum, one&nbsp;third of patients present with arrhythmias {10865004; 15360117}. Cardiac fibromas may cause obstruction or abnormal valvular function, leading to heart failure. Patients with asymptomatic tumours may present with a heart murmur, or the tumour may be identified incidentally on imaging.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67401,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cardiac fibromas (90%) occur in children, and they are the second most common cardiac tumour of childhood after rhabdomyoma. One&nbsp;third arise before the age of 1&nbsp;year, without sex predilection {17331235}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67402,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The <em>PTCH1</em> gene appears to play a role in at least a subset of cardiac fibromas. Mutations may be somatic, resulting in sporadic tumour formation, or within a syndromic context, associated with Gorlin syndrome (naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome). Gorlin syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by germline <em>PTCH1</em> mutation, characterized by multiple basal cell carcinomas, skeletal anomalies, distinct facies, and intracranial calcifications.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67403,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67404,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "<p>Cardiac fibromas are often large (mean: 50 mm), well-circumscribed, solitary tumours with a white whorled appearance. Although rare, multiple tumours have been reported. Calcification is common in this tumour and helps distinguish it from rhabdomyoma, particularly on imaging {1132411}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-02T09:30:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67405,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cardiac fibromas consist of benign fibroblasts within a variably collagenous background. Although the tumours are well circumscribed grossly, fibroblasts and collagen may infiltrate adjacent cardiac muscle histologically, but this does not correlate with recurrence risk. Cellularity frequently decreases with age of the patient, and the amount of collagen increases {19747857}. Microcalcifications and elastic fibres are variably present in patients of any age.</p>\r\n<p>The lesional cells exhibit myofibroblastic differentiation and are reactive with antibodies directed against SMA.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67406,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 487,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 444,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67407,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 444,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Demonstration of germline <em>PTCH1</em> mutation in Gorlin syndrome may be helpful in some cases {16572881}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 490,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 447,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67408,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 447,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Nodular collection of fibroblasts within a fibrotic stroma</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Microcalcifications</li>\r\n<li>Ventricular involvement</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 488,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 445,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67409,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 445,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 489,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 446,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67410,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 144,
        "headingId": 446,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Surgical excision is the treatment of choice for large tumours, and the recurrence rate is low, with excellent outcome. In asymptomatic patients, spontaneous regression is rare but has been reported {6033170; 7967668; 14667615}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:37:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]